{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The protocol authorizes reclamation via a BFT absence certificate, but the consensus participants have no causal mechanism to observe the crashed process’s local workspace. If evidence was completed locally yet the process crashed before publishing its hash to the ledger, the supermajority will truthfully certify that the ledger contains no such hash, allowing reclamation that silently displaces the sole copy of completed evidence remaining in the workspace.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The target explicitly substitutes recoverability for prevention, stating the protocol makes displaced evidence recoverable ‘rather than preventing displacement,’ directly contradicting the problem’s requirement of a guarantee never to silently displace completed evidence. It also supplies no causal mechanism—such as an atomic durability barrier or workspace handoff—to ensure completed evidence has actually exited the ephemeral workspace before a crash, so the evidence may reside solely there at crash time and be silently displaced by reclamation.","counterexample":null}]}